  "distractor_2": "Trust clients to include owner metadata; issue standard UUIDs; document missing auth; avoid rejecting cross-context requests, relying instead on client-side filtering and TTLs.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_2": "This is wrong because receivers MUST enforce authorization context and reject cross-context operations. Relying on client-supplied owner fields and client-side filtering allows unauthorized access and enumeration.",
  "thinking_process_2": "Chose a plausible approach that shifts enforcement to clients and uses common UUIDs; it's attractive for simpler servers but violates the spec's requirement for server-side context-binding and access control."
