  "distractor_2": "Provide host and client diagrams plus JSON Schema tests validating payload shapes and URIs, omitting JSON‑RPC error assertions.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_2": "Wrong because JSON Schema tests alone don't ensure servers emit the required standard JSON‑RPC error objects (-32002, -32603), proper binary encoding, URI validation, permissions checks, or declared notifications.",
  "thinking_process_2": "I offered a plausible contract-testing approach (JSON Schema) that matches the correct answer's structure but fails here because MCP requires explicit JSON‑RPC error assertions and other protocol guarantees; I avoided repeating a REST/OAuth substitution and adhered to the 17–18 word constraint."
}

{
  "distractor_2": "Require mutual TLS client certificates, perform server capability checks, enforce TLS session binding, enable OS-level auditd logging and stateful firewall rules.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_2": "This is wrong because transport/TLS and OS auditing secure connections and record events but don't provide MCP-level per-request JSON‑RPC binding, Prompt System/template enforcement, sampling/elicitation handlers, or the MCP loggingConsumer hooks needed for protocol-aware, auditable control and client-side mediation.",
  "thinking_process_2": "Chose a plausible security-first alternative (mTLS + OS logging) that someone might conflate with MCP controls. Emphasized transport and host-level controls rather than MCP client responsibilities like McpTransportContext binding, Prompt System enforcement, Roots/sampling capability checks, and MCP loggingConsumer integration. Ensured distractor wording mimics the checklist style of the correct answer and matched the 21-word constraint exactly."
}

{
  "distractor_2": "Provide client-side polyfills to emulate ui:// components on text-only hosts, converting templates into plain-text representations at runtime without server changes",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_2": "Shifting rendering to client-side polyfills assumes hosts can fully emulate UI semantics; polyfills are fragile, may misinterpret templates, and cannot replace mandatory server text fallbacks.",
  "thinking_process_2": "Used the source requirement that servers must provide text-only fallbacks. Devised an alternative that pushes rendering responsibility to hosts via polyfills—plausible elsewhere but incorrect here because it ignores server obligations and risks semantic loss. Ensured style mirrors the correct answer and crafted the distractor to be exactly twenty words."
}

{
  "distractor_2": "Require per-host namespaces inside client to isolate discovery results, caches, and request IDs when switching hosts.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_2": "Namespacing cached data still assumes the protocol session and server-side capabilities are interchangeable across hosts. MCP requires explicit re-discovery, capability negotiation, and session resets to ensure ordering, request semantics, and server-held state remain consistent; client-side namespaces alone can mask cross-environment contamination and race conditions.",
  "thinking_process_2": "The docs list multiple base hosts, so a plausible solution is isolating client caches per host. I chose namespacing as a different plausible but incorrect strategy because it treats protocol state as purely client-side cacheable data. The real issue is protocol-level session and capability negotiation that must be explicitly reset or re-run when changing environments."
}
